#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135018 :tutorial/content "An **angle** is formed whenever two rays meet at a point. \n\nThe common point shared by the rays is called the **vertex**, and the rays themselves are called the **sides** of the angle.\n\n![](images/ca/caa6fe6d949902d06c155f109fc2202bc4adbfc0c324591100a56337f6841279.png)\n\nConsider the following angle, made up of the rays $\\overrightarrow{{\\color{blue}{A}}B}$ and $\\overrightarrow{{\\color{blue}{A}}C}.$\n\n![](images/d4/d4a10fda5cbf508754812bb114e80de3f3aad529c54d6b82538284bda58cd737.png)\n\nThere are several different ways of labeling this angle.\n\n- One way to write this angle is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\angle C\\!{\\color{blue}{A}}B\n$$\n\n \nThe symbol $\\angle$ means \"angle.\" Notice that the vertex ${\\color{blue}{A}}$ is in the middle.\n\n- We can also swap the order in which we write the endpoints. For example,\n\n$$\n\\angle B{\\color{blue}{A}}C.\n$$\n\n \nNotice that the vertex ${\\color{blue}{A}}$ is still in the middle.\n\n- To save space, we sometimes write $\\angle {\\color{blue}{A}}.$\n\n- Finally, instead of using the $\\angle$ symbol, we can write $\\widehat{C{\\color{blue}{A}}B}$ or $\\widehat{B{\\color{blue}{A}}C}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "991904e0-131d-59ea-b73b-080a9980bb0b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2176 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135150 :tutorial/content "The **measure** of an angle is the amount of \"turn\" from one side of the angle to the other. \n\nWe measure angles in **degrees**, which have the symbol $^\\circ.$ \n\nLet's look at a few examples of angles with their measures.\n\n![](images/7c/7c8a1fb7268052f44c88734607cd8e38ae8b57b924ec9fb65a6cb8918c60266f.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The more \"turn\" there is, the larger the angle's measure.\n\n- A corner angle (like the angle $C$ above) has a measure of $90^\\circ.$ An angle with measure $90^\\circ$ is called a **right angle.**\n\nWe use the letter $m$ to represent the measure of the angle. For example,\n\n$$\nm \\angle C = 90^\\circ.\n$$\n\nWe'd read this as \"the measure of the angle $C$ is $90$ degrees.\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "90b2ad8a-62aa-50c2-a1d2-0ff4f1a62e0a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6649 :tutorial/title "Angle Measures"}]}
